<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Futa Toro</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Fouta Toro</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Futa Toro is a region along the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> river, on the border of present-day Senegal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a>, home of the Fulbe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toucouleur">Toucouleur</a> peoples. It was the seat of the Denianke kingdom from the 16th century, until Muslim clerics overthrew it and founded an Islamic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">imamate</a> in 1776. Futa Toro was the homeland of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-hadj-umar-tall">El Hadj Umar Tall</a> and came under French rule in the late 19th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/futa-toro.jpg" alt="Futa Toro" width="320" height="159" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nicolas Eynaud · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Africa_de_l%27O%C3%A8st_(fin_s%C3%A8gle_XVIII).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
